<h1><a name="_Toc412720785"><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>4.04    BUDGETING
AND REPORTING SYSTEM</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal align=center style='text-align:center'><b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>II.
 BUDGETING SYSTEM</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.04.020         Definitions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal align=center style='text-align:center'><b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>V.
 CAPITAL IMPROVEMENT PROGRAM</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.04.246         CIP
lease based projects - phases - procedures.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal align=center style='text-align:center'><b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>II. 
BUDGETING SYSTEM</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.04.020 
Definitions.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  The definitions in this section apply throughout this
chapter unless the context clearly requires otherwise.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A. 
&quot;Acquisition phase&quot; means the time during which activities associated
with acquisition or surplus and sale of real property, property rights or the
acquisition of improvements through direct purchase or capitalized lease
agreements occur.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B. 
&quot;Adopted&quot; means approval by council motion or ordinance.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C. 
&quot;Agency&quot; means a county office, officer, institution whether
educational, correctional or other, department, division, board commission,
except as otherwise provided in this chapter.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  &quot;Allocation&quot;
means a part of a lump sum appropriation that is designated for expenditure by either
a specific organization unit or for specific purposes, or both.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  &quot;Allotment&quot;
means a part of an appropriation that may be encumbered or expended during an
allotment period.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            F.  &quot;Allotment
period&quot; means a period of less than a fiscal year in length during which
an allotment is effective.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            G. 
&quot;Allotment plan&quot; means a fiscal management plan that divides a county
agency's program element budget into quarterly increments, reflecting the
cyclical or seasonal pattern of expenditures, for the purpose of identifying
over and under expenditures throughout the year.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            H.  &quot;Appropriations&quot;
means an authorization granted by the council to make expenditures and to incur
obligations for specific purposes.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            I.  &quot;Appropriation
ordinance&quot; means the ordinance that establishes the legal level of
appropriation for a fiscal year.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            J.  &quot;Art&quot;
means funds budgeted for the one percent for art program under K.C.C. chapter
4.40 or as otherwise provided by ordinance for a public art program.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            K.  &quot;Budget&quot;
means a proposed plan of expenditures for a given period or purpose and the
proposed means for financing these expenditures.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            L. 
&quot;Budget detail plan&quot; means the council's proposed spending plan for
the operational budgets of all agencies detailed at the section level and
attached to the adopted appropriation ordinance or as modified by the
most-recent supplemental appropriation ordinance.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            M.  &quot;Budget
document&quot; means a formal, written, comprehensive financial program
presented by the executive to the council, including an electronic database
with revenues and expenditures for all county agencies at the lowest
organization levels and all summary levels provided in the general ledger
system, balanced to the financial plans and the appropriation ordinance, fee
ordinances, motions related to proposed levy rates to comply with chapter 36.40
RCW and cost-of-living adjustment ordinance proposed by the executive.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            N. 
&quot;Budget message&quot; means a formal oral presentation by the executive to
the council that explains the budget in terms of goals to be accomplished and
how the budget relates to the Comprehensive Plan.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            O.  &quot;Capital
improvement plan&quot; means a plan that establishes the capital improvements
required to implement an approved operational master plan.  This plan should
extend over a minimum period of six years to define long-range capital
improvement requirements and the annual capital improvements budget for a user
agency.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  The
capital improvement plan shall include the following elements, where
applicable:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                a.  general
program requirements that define the development scope for specific sites or
facilities;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                b.  general
space and construction standards;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                c.  prototype
floor plans and prototype facility designs for standard improvements;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                d.  space
requirements based on the adopted county space plan;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                e.  initial,
and life-cycle cost, of alternative facilities and locations including lease
and lease/purchase approaches;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                f.  approximate
location of planned capital improvements;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                g.  general
scope and estimated cost of infrastructure; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                h.  a
schedule, that extends over a minimum of six years, for the implementation of
projects included in capital improvement plans, based on overall user agency
priorities and projected available revenue.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  The user
agency shall prepare the elements of the plan in subsection O.1. a., d., f.,
and h. of this section.  The implementing agency shall prepare the elements of
this plan in subsection O.1. b., c., e., and g. of this section.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  The
six-year budget schedule included in the capital improvement plan shall be
updated annually in conjunction with the capital budget adoption process.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            P. 
&quot;Capital project” means a project with a scope that includes one or more
of the following elements, all related to a capital asset:  acquisition of
either a site or existing structure, or both; program or site master planning;
design and environmental analysis; construction; major equipment acquisition;
reconstruction; demolition; or major alteration.  &quot;Capital project&quot;
includes a:  project program plan; scope; budget by phase; and schedule.  The
project budget and phases of a project shall be prepared or managed by the
implementing agency.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            Q. 
&quot;CIP&quot; means capital improvement program.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            R.  &quot;CIP
exceptions notification&quot; means, except for major maintenance reserve fund,
roads, solid waste, surface water management and wastewater CIP projects, a
letter filed with the clerk of the council for distribution to the chair of the
budget and fiscal management committee, or its successor committee, which
describes changes to an adopted CIP project's scope or schedule, or both, or
total project cost and, with the exception of schedule changes, shall be sent
in advance of any action.  For major maintenance reserve fund CIP projects,
&quot;exceptions notification&quot; means a letter filed with the clerk of the
council for distribution to the chair of the budget and fiscal management
committee, or its successor committee, that describes changes of fifteen
percent or more to an adopted CIP project's scope or schedule, or both, or
total project costs and, with the exception of schedule changes, shall be sent
in advance of any action.  For road CIP projects, &quot;exceptions
notification&quot; means a letter filed with the clerk of the council for
distribution to the </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>chair
of the transportation committee, or its successor committee, that describes
changes of fifteen percent or more to an adopted CIP project's scope or
schedule, or both, or total project costs and, with the exception of schedule
changes, shall be sent in advance of any action.  For wastewater, solid waste and
surface water management CIP projects, &quot;exceptions notification&quot;
means a letter filed with the clerk of the council for distribution to the
chair of the budget and fiscal management committee, or its successor
committee, and to the chair of the utilities committee, or its successor
committee, which describes changes of fifteen percent or more to an adopted CIP
project's scope or schedule, or both, or total project costs and, with the
exception of schedule changes, shall be sent in advance of any action.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            S.  &quot;Close-out
phase&quot; means the time during which the administrative processes and
associated accounting activities to close out all contracts occurs.  The
close-out phase follows final acceptance and may include multiyear monitoring.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            T.  &quot;Council&quot;
means the metropolitan King County council.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            U.  &quot;Deficit&quot;
means the excess of expenditures over revenues during an accounting period, or
an accumulation of such excesses over a period of years.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            V. 
&quot;Director&quot; means the budget director in the executive's office.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            W.  &quot;Executive&quot;
means the King County executive, as defined by Article 3 of the King County
Charter.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            X.  &quot;Expenditures&quot;
means, where the accounts are kept on the accrual basis or the modified accrual
basis, the cost of goods delivered or services rendered, whether paid or
unpaid, including expenses, provisions for debt retirement not reported as a
liability of the fund from which retired, and capital outlays.  Where the
accounts are kept on the cash basis, &quot;expenditures&quot; means actual cash
disbursements for these purposes.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            Y. 
&quot;Final design phase&quot; means the time during which design is completed,
permits and other permissions are secured so that the project, or staged
elements of the project consistent with the project management plan, can
proceed to implementation.  Final design phase also includes development of a
final cost estimate, plans, specifications and a bid package.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            Z.  &quot;Financial
plan&quot; means a summary by fund of planned revenues and expenditures,
reserves and undesignated fund balance.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            AA. 
&quot;Fiscal period&quot; means a calendar year or a biennium.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            BB.  &quot;Fund&quot;
an independent fiscal and accounting entity with a self-balancing set of
accounts recording either cash or other resources, or both, together with
related liabilities, obligations, reserves and equities that are segregated for
the purpose of carrying on specific activities or attaining certain objectives
in accordance with special regulations, restrictions or limitations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            CC.  &quot;Fund
balance&quot; means the excess of the assets of a fund over its liabilities and
reserves except in the case of funds subject to budgetary accounting where, before
the end of a fiscal period, it represents the excess of the fund assets and
estimated revenues for the period over its liabilities, reserves and
appropriations for the fiscal period.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            DD. 
&quot;General facility major maintenance emergent need contingency
project&quot; means an appropriation to provide contingent budget authority for
emergent needs within major maintenance reserve fund CIP projects.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            EE. 
&quot;Implementation phase&quot; means the time during which a project is
constructed or implemented.  &quot;Implementation phase&quot; also includes the
testing, inspection, adjustment, correction and certification of facilities and
systems to ensure that the project performs as specified.  The implementation phase
begins with the notice to proceed for the construction contract and ends with
final acceptance of the project, unless otherwise specified in grant or
regulatory requirements.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            FF.  &quot;Implementing
agency&quot; means the appropriate department and division responsible for the
administration of CIP projects.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            GG.  &quot;Lapse&quot;
of an appropriation means an automatic termination of an appropriation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            HH.  &quot;Major
maintenance reserve fund CIP project&quot; means any major maintenance reserve
fund CIP project that is allocated in the adopted six-year major maintenance
reserve fund CIP and is appropriated at the major maintenance reserve fund
level in accordance with K.C.C. 4.04.265* or is a high-risk project under
K.C.C. 4.04.245**.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            II.  &quot;Major
widening project&quot; means any roads CIP project adding at least one through lane
in each direction.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            JJ.  &quot;Object
of expenditure&quot; means a grouping of expenditures on the basis of goods and
services purchased, such as salary and wages.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            KK.  &quot;Open
space non-bond fund project&quot; means an open space project that is allocated
in the adopted six-year open space CIP and is appropriated at the open space
non-bond fund number 3522 level in accordance with K.C.C. 4.04.300***.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            LL.  &quot;Operational
master plan&quot; means a comprehensive plan for an agency setting forth how
the organization will operate now and in the future.  An operational master
plan shall include the analysis of alternatives and their life cycle costs to
accomplish defined goals and objectives, performance measures, projected
workload, needed resources, implementation schedules and general cost
estimates.  The operational master plan shall also address how the organization
would respond in the future to changed conditions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            MM. 
&quot;Planning phase&quot; means the time during which identification and
development of project need and potential alternatives, evaluation of technical
and economic feasibility and development of a rough-order-of-magnitude total
project cost estimate occurs.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            NN. 
&quot;Preliminary design phase&quot; means the time during which when
evaluation and analysis of potential project alternatives occurs, and the preferred
alternative is selected and designed sufficiently to establish a project
baseline, at thirty to forty percent design.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            OO.  &quot;Program&quot;
means the definition of resources and efforts committed to satisfying a public
need. The extent to which the public need is satisfied is measured by the
effectiveness of the process in fulfilling the needs as expressed in explicit
objectives.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            PP.  “Project
program plan” means a plan, primarily in written narrative form, that describes
the overall development concept and scope of work for a building, group of
buildings or other facilities at a particular site.  The complexity of the
project program plan will vary based upon the size and difficulty of the
program for a particular site.  When the plan includes projects that are phased
over time, each phase shall have an updated project program plan prepared by
the user agency before project implementation.  The project program plan shall
be prepared by the user agency with assistance from the implementing agency. 
The program plan describes the user agency program requirements for a specific
building or site; provides the basis for these requirements; and identifies
when funds for the implementation of the capital projects will be provided. 
The program plan shall elaborate on the general program information provided in
the operational master plan and the capital improvement plan.  The plan shall
also describe user agency programs, how these programs would fit and function
on the site, and the general recommendation of the user agency regarding the
appearance of the building or site.  The plan shall indicate when a site master
plan is required for a project.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            QQ.  &quot;Public
need&quot; means those public services found to be required to maintain the
health, safety and well-being of the general citizenry.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            RR.  &quot;Quarterly
management and budget report&quot; means a report prepared quarterly by the director
for major operating and capital funds, that:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  Presents
executive revisions to the adopted financial plan or plans; </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  Identifies
significant deviations in agency workload from approved levels; </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  Identifies
potential future supplemental appropriations with a brief discussion of the
rationale for each potential supplemental;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4. 
Identifies significant variances in revenue estimates;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              5.  Reports
information for each appropriation unit on the number of filled and vacant
full-time equivalent and term-limited temporary positions and the number of
temporary employees;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              6.  Includes
the budget allotment plan information required under K.C.C. 4.04.060****; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              7. 
Describes progress towards transitioning potential annexation areas to cities.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            SS.  &quot;Reappropriation&quot;
means authorization granted by the council to expend the appropriation for the
previous fiscal year for capital programs only.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            TT.  &quot;Regulations&quot;
means the policies, standards and requirements, stated in writing, designed to
carry out the purposes of this chapter, as issued by the executive and having
the force and effect of law.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            UU.  &quot;Revenue&quot;
means the addition to assets that does not increase any liability, does not represent
the recovery of an expenditure, does not represent the cancellation of certain
liabilities on a decrease in assets and does not represent a contribution to
fund capital in enterprise and intragovernmental service funds.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            VV.  &quot;Roads
CIP project&quot; means roads capital projects that are allocated in the
adopted six-year roads CIP and are appropriated at the roads CIP fund level in
accordance with K.C.C. 4.04.270***** or a high-risk project under K.C.C.
4.04.245**.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            WW.  &quot;Scope
change&quot; means, except for major maintenance reserve fund, roads, solid
waste, surface water management, and wastewater CIP projects, that a CIP
project's total project cost increases by ten percent or by fifty thousand
dollars, whichever is less.  For major maintenance reserve fund, roads, solid
waste, surface water management or wastewater CIP projects, &quot;scope
change&quot; means the total project cost increases by fifteen percent.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            XX. 
&quot;Section&quot; means an agency's budget unit comprised of a particular
project, program or line of business as described in Ordinance 16445, Section 5,
for the 2010 budget or for all subsequent budgets as described in the budget
detail plan for the previous fiscal period as attached to the adopted
appropriation ordinance or as modified by the most-recent supplemental
appropriation ordinance.  This definition is not intended to create an
organizational structure for any agency.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            YY.  “Site
master plan” means a plan prepared by the implementing agency, with input from
the user agency, that describes, illustrates and defines the capital
improvements required to provide user agency program elements.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  The site
master plan shall include preliminary information regarding, at a minimum:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                a.  site
analysis, including environmental constraints;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                b.  layout,
illustration and description of all capital improvements;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                c.  project
scopes and budgets;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                d.  project
phasing; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                e.  operating
and maintenance requirements.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  The site
master plan shall be approved by the user agency and the implementing agency before
submittal to the executive and council for approval.<a name=elva></a></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            ZZ.  &quot;Solid
waste CIP project&quot; means a solid waste project that is allocated in the
adopted six-year solid waste CIP and is appropriated at the solid waste CIP
fund level in accordance with K.C.C. 4.04.273****** or is a high-risk project
under K.C.C. 4.04.245**.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            AAA.  &quot;Surface
water management CIP project&quot; means a surface water management project
that is allocated in the adopted six-year surface water management CIP and is
appropriated at the surface water management CIP fund level in accordance with K.C.C.
4.04.275******* or is a high-risk project under K.C.C. 4.04.245**.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            BBB.  &quot;User
agency&quot; means the appropriate department, division, office or section to
be served by any proposed CIP project.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            CCC.  &quot;Wastewater
asset management projects&quot; means the wastewater capital projects
identified and intended by the wastewater treatment division to extend and
optimize the useful life of wastewater treatment assets, including facilities,
structures, pipelines and equipment.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            DDD.  &quot;Wastewater
CIP project&quot; means wastewater capital projects that are allocated in the
adopted six-year wastewater CIP and are appropriated at the wastewater CIP fund
level in accordance with K.C.C. 4.04.280******** or is a high-risk project
under K.C.C. 4.04.245.  (Ord. 20082 § 26, 2026:  Ord. 17229 </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>1,
2011:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 16960 § 14, 2010:  </span><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 16764 </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>6,
2010:  Ord. 16764 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 16445 § 1, 2008: </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> Ord. 15545 § 2, 2006:  Ord.
14811 § 2, 2003:  Ord. 14743 § 2, 2003:  Ord. 14561 § 12, 2002:  Ord. 14452 §
1, 2002:  Ord. 14122 § 1, 2001:  Ord. 13035 § 1, 1998:  Ord. 12076 § 2, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Reviser's
notes:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>*K.C.C.
4.04.265 was repealed by Ordinance 17929 § 40, 2014.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>**K.C.C.
4.04.245 was rerecodified as K.C.C. 4A.130.010 by Ordinance 17930 § 16, 2014.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>***K.C.C.
4.04.300 was repealed by Ordinance 17929 § 46, 2014.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>****K.C.C.
4.04.060 was recodified as K.C.C. 4A.100.100 by Ordinance 17929 § 24, 2014.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>*****K.C.C.
4.04.270 was repealed by Ordinance 17929 § 42, 2014.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>******K.C.C.
4.04.273 was repealed by Ordinance 17929 § 43, 2014.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>*******K.C.C.
4.04.275 was repealed by Ordinance 17929 § 44, 2014.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>********K.C.C.
4.04.280 was repealed by Ordinance 17929 § 45, 2014.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal align=center style='text-align:center'><b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>V. 
CAPITAL IMPROVEMENT PROGRAM</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.04.246  CIP
lease based projects - phases - procedures.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  For
purposes of this section, &quot;lease-based project&quot; is as defined in K.C.C.
4.04.245*.  The phases of a lease-based project are the planning,
predevelopment and lease phases.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  The
planning phase is the time during which the county develops the scope of work
including an estimate of project size, construction cost, and tenant
improvements.  The procurement process for the developer is completed in this
phase.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  The
predevelopment phase is the time during which the county in conjunction with
the developer identifies and analyzes potential alternatives, selects the
preferred alternative and evaluates the technical and economic feasibility of
the project.  The preferred alternative is engineered to approximately thirty
percent design. The draft lease agreement is agreed to in principle by the
developer and the executive at the completion of the predevelopment phase.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  The
lease phase is when the executive requests council approval to enter into a
lease agreement for the project.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  When
submitting a capital budget appropriation ordinance or an additional or amended
capital budget appropriation ordinance to the council that encompasses work to
be performed in the planning phase of a lease-based project, the executive
shall provide as supporting data a cost estimate that details the anticipated
cost for each major task required to complete the predevelopment phase.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  When
submitting a capital budget appropriation ordinance or an additional or amended
capital budget appropriation ordinance to the council that encompasses work to
be performed in the predevelopment phase of a lease-based project, the
executive shall provide as supporting data an estimate of project size,
construction cost and tenant improvements, and a description of performance
requirements and space needs.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  When
submitting an ordinance requesting council approval to enter into a lease
agreement for a lease-based project, the executive shall provide supporting
data as follows:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  Identify
design criteria;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  Provide
a financial analysis of the project;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  Provide
the draft lease agreement agreed to by the executive and developer;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  Provide
the development team's pro forma cost plan for the design and construction of
the project;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              5.  Provide
a description and an analysis of the feasible alternatives considered, and
identify the recommended alternative;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              6.  Provide
planned schedule that shows the anticipated start and finish dates for each
major task, consistent with the development team's pro forma cost plan;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              7.  Identify
stakeholders;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              8.  Identify
regulatory requirements;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              9.  Report
variance of predevelopment phase major task performance from the schedule and
cost estimate provided for the predevelopment phase.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  For any
item required by subsections B. through D. of this section that the executive
does not provide with the appropriation request, the executive shall provide a
detailed explanation of why it cannot be provided and, if the item is to be
provided later, identify the date by which the item will be provided.  (Ord.
16764 § 3, 2010).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>*Reviser's
note:  K.C.C. 4.04.245 was rerecodified as K.C.C. 4A.130.010 by Ordinance 17930
§ 16, 2014.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc319916835"></a><a name="_Toc319916897"></a><a
name="_Toc412720786"><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>4.10    INVESTMENT OF FUNDS</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.10.010      Definitions</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.10.040      Maximum available for
investment.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.10.050      Executive finance committee.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.10.060      Reports on investments.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.10.070      Investment earnings, losses and
penalties.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.10.080      Report to the council.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.10.090      Pooled investments.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.10.100      Repealer.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.10.110      New funds.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.10.120      Ratification of prior
investments.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.10.130      Administrative rules.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.10.150      Outstanding investments.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.10.010  Definitions.</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 
As used in this chapter, the following terms shall have the following meanings:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  &quot;Manager&quot;
means manager of the finance and business operations division.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  &quot;First
tier fund&quot;:  Each county fund listed or described as a first tier fund in
K.C.C. chapter 4.08*.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  &quot;Fund
manager&quot; means that person holding or exercising the powers of the
position or office specified in K.C.C. chapter 4.08* as the manager for each
fund and such persons to whom the fund manager has delegated duties and responsibilities
as provided in K.C.C. chapter 4.08*.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  &quot;Residual
treasury cash&quot; means any cash in the custody or control of the finance and
business operations division as to which no investment directive under the
first paragraph of RCW 36.29.020, as now or hereafter amended, has been
received by the manager of the finance and business operations division. 
Residual treasury cash includes county cash for which the fund manager has not
directed a specific fund investment pursuant to this chapter.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  &quot;Second
tier fund&quot; means a fund that is not to be invested for its own benefit under
the first paragraph of RCW 36.29.020 and listed as a second tier fund in K.C.C.
chapter 4.08*.  (Ord. 14561 § 19, 2002:  Ord. 14199 § 53, 2001:  Ord. 12076 §
33, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>*Reviser's
note:  K.C.C. chapter 4.08 was recodified as K.C.C. chapter 4A.200 by Ordinance
17527.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>4.10.040  Maximum
available for investment.</b>  For each first tier fund designated in K.C.C. chapter
4.08*, the maximum amount of funds available but not required for immediate
expenditure which are to be invested pursuant to the first paragraph of RCW
36.29.020, as now or hereafter amended, is to be determined by the fund manager
prospectively taking into consideration the need to maintain sufficient cash
liquidity in the fund to meet current expenditure requirements.  Each fund
manager shall make such a determination for each fund no less often than
weekly, nor more often than daily, and shall promptly advise the manager of the
finance and business operations division, in such manner and subject to such
reasonable administrative constraints as the manager of the finance and
business operations division shall establish, if there are funds to be
invested, to invest such amounts from each fund, informing the manager of the
finance and business operations division of the amount and maximum length of
maturity appropriate for each investment, and such amounts are hereby
authorized for investment  under the first paragraph of RCW 36.29.020, as now
or hereafter  amended.  Such investments shall be made and the lengths of their
maturities selected by the manager of the finance and business operations
division in consultation with the executive finance committee.  Consultation
with the executive finance committee shall include, at a minimum, full
disclosure of average invested and residual cash for each county fund.  Such
investments shall not negate or affect the authority of the manager of the
finance and business operations division, under the guidance of the executive
finance committee, to include the retained cash balance in the fund as part of
the residual treasury cash invested under the second paragraph of RCW 36.29.020,
as now or hereafter amended, for the benefit of the county Current Expense
Fund.  (Ord. 14199 § 54, 2001:  Ord. 12076 § 34, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>*Reviser's
note:  K.C.C. chapter 4.08 was recodified as K.C.C. chapter 4A.200 by Ordinance
17527.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>4.10.050  Executive
finance committee.</b>  The executive finance committee is hereby confirmed as
being the &quot;county finance committee,&quot; referred to in RCW 36.29.020
and RCW 36.48.070, and shall be composed of the following:  the manager of the
finance and business operations division; a representative of the executive's
office; the director of department of executive services; and the chair of the
county council.  The executive finance committee shall be responsible for
directing the manager of the finance and business operations division in
determining the maximum prudent extent to which residual treasury cash shall be
invested pursuant to RCW 36.29.020 and this chapter.  Actions of the committee
shall be by majority vote except when the chair of the council determines such
action constitutes a policy determination, as opposed to an administrative
determination, which should be referred to the council.  The chair of the
council may defer action on the proposal until the council makes such policy
determination regarding the proposed action. (Ord. 20082 § 27, 2026:  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord.
16960 § 21 2010:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 14561 § 20, 2002:  Ord. 14199 § 55, 2001:  Ord.
12076 § 35, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>4.10.060  Reports
on investments.</b>  The investment instruments in which county funds shall be
invested shall be selected solely by the manager of the finance and business
operations division or designee and fully reported to the executive finance
committee on a monthly basis at a minimum.  Any losses on investments including
all investments of the county treasury shall be reported by the manager of the
finance and business operations division to all members of the executive
finance committee immediately upon discovery.  Investments shall be chosen from
those which are now or may hereafter be legally permitted, with the aim of
maximizing return to the county while safeguarding county funds, providing the
liquidity needed to meet county obligations in timely fashion, and complying
with such other county policy directives as now exist or may be hereafter
adopted.  (</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 18618 </span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>
105, 2017:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 14199 § 56, 2001:  Ord. 7112 § 6, 1985).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>4.10.070  Investment
earnings, losses and penalties.</b>  Investment earnings and losses and any
penalties for premature liquidation shall be allocated as follows: </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  In the
case of first tier funds, for those specific fund investments directed by the
fund manager, an investment service fee equal to the maximum now or hereafter
authorized by state law shall be deposited in the county current expense fund,
and the balance of the maturing or liquidated investment, plus earnings if any,
shall be deposited in the specific fund out of which the investment originated.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            As an
alternative to premature liquidation of these specific fund investments, the
executive finance committee may provide, on appropriate terms and conditions,
for temporary interfund borrowing to cover unforeseen cash liquidity needs, and
may provide for interfund purchases, at then market value, of investments in
order to avoid penalties, provided that, the manager of the finance and
business operations division shall report to the executive finance committee
any temporary interfund borrowing made to avoid liquidation of any investment
instrument if such liquidation would have resulted in a loss of principal or
interest.  Terms and conditions should specify an interest rate and schedule of
repayment.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  For all
other county funds, and for residual treasury cash investments attributable to
first tier funds, all earnings and losses and any penalties for premature
liquidation shall be deducted from or deposited in the county current expense
fund and used for general county purposes.  (Ord. 14199 § 57, 2001:  Ord. 7112
§ 7, 1985).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>4.10.080  Report
to the council.</b>  The executive finance committee shall report to the county
council quarterly the average residual cash and investment balances of each
first tier fund, the amount of investment earnings received by each first tier
fund, the specific fund investments outstanding at the end of the quarter for
each first tier fund, and the amount retained in that fund at the end of the
quarter as part of the residual treasury cash.  (Ord. 7112 § 8, 1985).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>4.10.090 
Pooled investments.</b>  At the direction of the executive finance committee,
with the agreement of the fund manager, the manager of the finance and business
operations division or designee, may pool monies for specific fund investments
with other monies directed for specific fund investments by a fund manager
under the first paragraph of RCW 36.29.020, as now or hereafter amended, monies
in the residual treasury cash and monies directed for investment by other
municipal corporations.  Interest earnings and any losses shall be apportioned
pro rata, after payment of investment service fees to the county current
expense fund, to each of the funds participating in the pooled investment.  (</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord.
18618 </span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 106, 2017:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 14199 § 58, 2001: 
Ord. 7112 § 9, 1985).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>4.10.100  Repealer.</b> 
King County Resolution 36165 is hereby repealed, except that it shall continue
to govern the investment of county funds consisting primarily of proceeds from
the issuance of bonds or other county obligations authorized (whether or not
yet issued) prior to January 1, 1985, and shall take precedence over Ordinance
7112 as to such funds in case of irreconcilable conflict.  (Ord. 7112 § 10,
1985).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            4.10.110  New funds.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.  Whenever a new county fund
shall be created, unless it is a first tier fund, consideration shall be given
to whether it should be a first tier fund, and the council shall consider the
recommendation of the executive in this regard within forty-five days of
receiving such recommendation.  Any fund  as to which no specific action is or
has been taken within forty-five days to authorize specific fund investments
under the first paragraph of RCW 36.29.020, as now or hereafter amended, shall
be a second tier fund.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  Whenever a new county fund is
established with direction that all surplus moneys in the fund be invested for
the benefit of that fund, or when such direction is given in connection with
any county fund, such direction shall be deemed to mean only that monies
available but not required for immediate expenditure shall be invested for that
fund according to the procedures and limitations contained in this chapter, and
such direction shall not negate or affect the authority of the manager of the
finance and business operations division, under the guidance of the executive
finance committee, to include the retained cash balance in that fund as part of
the residual treasury cash invested under the second paragraph of RCW
36.29.020, as now or hereafter amended, for the benefit of the general fund;
except that if specific negative reference is made to this chapter and
direction is expressly given that the Current Expense Fund shall not receive
any earnings attributable to the fund in question, then and only then shall
that particular fund be entitled to its proportionate share of any earnings
resulting from residual treasury cash.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            C.  In case direction shall be
given that certain moneys in a fund be invested for the benefit of that fund,
and no fund manager be provided for, then the director of the county department
or office primarily responsible for expenditures from that fund shall be the
fund manager.  (Ord. 17527 § 4, 2013:  Ord. 14199 § 59, 2001:  Ord. 12646 § 2,
1996: Ord. 12076 § 36, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            4.10.120  Ratification of prior
investments.  </span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>All
prior actions of the executive finance committee and the manager of the finance
and business operations division taken in connection with investment directives
and policies, investment decisions, and the allocation of investment earnings,
as they relate to the investment of county funds, are hereby ratified.  (</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord.
18618 </span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 107, 2017:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 14199 § 60, 2001:  Ord. 12076 § 37, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>4.10.130  Administrative rules.</b> 
The manager of the finance and business operations division shall promulgate
administrative rules pursuant to K.C.C. chapter 2.98 to implement this
chapter.  The rules shall be approved by the executive finance committee. 
(Ord. 14199 § 61, 2001:  Ord. 7112 § 13, 1985).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            4.10.150  Outstanding investments.</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>  In the event that
there are any investments outstanding on January 1, 1985, which have been made
for the benefit of a county fund (the &quot;originating fund&quot;) that will
no longer, upon January 1, 1985, be entitled to retain investment earnings,
then the earning or loss from the investment shall, upon receipt or maturity,
be divided proportionately between the originating fund and the current expense
fund according to the number of calendar days such investment was outstanding
before and on or after January 1, 1985.  In such cases, the current expense
fund shall receive, from the originating fund's share of investment earnings,
the maximum investment service fee now or hereafter allowed by state law. 
(Ord. 7112 § 15, 1985).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc319916838"></a><a name="_Toc319916900"></a><a
name="_Toc412720787"><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>4.14    BIDDING PROCEDURES
FOR FINANCIAL SERVICES CONTRACTS</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.14.010      Policy.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.14.020      Frequency of financial services
solicitation process.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.14.030      Financial service need survey.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.14.040      Development of and evaluation
criteria for financial services specifications - review and approval by motion
- evaluation - community reinvestment plans required for proposals - rejection
of nonresponsive bids - plan for outreach upon award of contracts.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.14.048      Annual certification of
county's commercial banking institution.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.14.050      Approved specifications
forwarding - preproposal conference.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.14.070      County financial service
contracts - waiver prohibited - extension and termination - actions upon
breach.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.14.080      Financial services contract
with another commercial banking institution that is not the county's main
financial services commercial banking institution for local government
convenience.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.14.090      Chapter not basis for proviate
right of action.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.14.010  Policy.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  It is a
requirement of Washington state law [and]* the policy of King County that
financial services provided to the county by commercial banking institutions be
provided as a result of open competitive procurement processes.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Financial
services provided to the county by commercial banking institutions may only be
provided by commercial banking institutions that have a demonstrated history of
and ongoing commitment to socially responsible banking practices.  (Ord. 19637
§ 2, 2023:  Ord. 17756 § 1, 2014:  Ord. 12076 § 39, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><a name="_Hlk140069097"><b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>*Reviser’s
note: Language added but not underlined in Ordinance 19637. See K.C.C. 1.24.075</span></b></a></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>4.14.020  Frequency
of financial services solicitation process.</b>  Except for banks serving the
needs of special districts as set forth in K.C.C. 4.14.080 and during a state
of emergency declared under chapter K.C.C. 12.52.030, in accordance with this
chapter, at least every seven years, the county shall competitively solicit the
financial services required by the county from qualifying commercial banking
institutions.  Any extension of a financial services contract due to a state of
emergency shall be no longer than two years unless approved by ordinance..  (Ord.
19637 § 3, 2023:  Ord. 19297 § 2, 2021:  Ord. 17756 § 2, 2014:  Ord. 14484 § 1,
2002:  Ord. 12076 § 40, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.14.030  Financial
service need survey.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  The </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif'>manager of the finance and business operations division <span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>shall conduct a survey of the financial services
needs of the county prior to initiating the procurement process.  The survey
shall be reviewed and updated prior to each subsequent procurement process. 
(Ord. 14199 § 62, 2001:  Ord. 12076 § 41, 1995).</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.14.040  Development
of and evaluation criteria for financial services specifications - review and
approval by motion - evaluation - community reinvestment plans required for
proposals - rejection of nonresponsive bids - plan for outreach upon award of
contracts</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  The </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>manager of the finance
and business operations division <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>shall
develop the financial services specifications stating the county’s needs and
the responsible banking qualification requirements of the county, and the
evaluation criteria to be used to score proposals submitted by commercial
banking institutions.</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  The specifications
and evaluation criteria shall be reviewed and approved by the council by motion
before the manager may solicit proposals to provide financial services to the
county from commercial banking institutions.  The executive shall
electronically file the motion to approve the specifications and evaluation
criteria no later than March 31, with the clerk of the council, who shall
retain the original and provide an electronic copy to all councilmembers, the
council chief of staff, the chief policy officer, and the lead staff for the
budget and fiscal management committee, or its successor.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  Each
commercial banking institution submitting a proposal will be evaluated for its
financial health in accordance with the specifications and evaluation criteria.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  The
specifications shall include a minimum requirement that the commercial banking
institution has a current Community Reinvestment Act rating of
&quot;outstanding&quot; from the U.S. Office of the Comptroller of the
Currency, the U.S. Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, and the
Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation.  The “outstanding” rating shall apply to
a rating assessment area that includes the geographical boundaries of King
County.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  Each commercial
banking institution submitting a proposal is required to submit a community
reinvestment plan describing past performance and proposed initiatives within
King County related to lending, investing, and providing community banking
services to traditionally underserved and disadvantaged communities.  The
community reinvestment plan shall be part of the evaluation process for
awarding a contract.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            F.  A
community reinvestment plan should summarize, as well as supplement,
information that each commercial banking institution is required to submit to the
U.S. Office of the Comptroller of the Currency, the U.S. Board of Governors of
the Federal Reserve Bank, and the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation as part
of its most-recent Community Reinvestment Act rating process for an assessment
area that includes King County.  All data and reporting within the plan shall
focus on actions the commercial banking institution is taking, or planning to
take, to improve access to bank services and to increase opportunities for
building assets and providing credit in low-income and racially diverse areas
throughout King County.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            G.  A
community reinvestment plan should highlight performance over the previous
three years and include goals for the duration of the contract period that fall
into three categories: lending, investing and services.  The plan shall also
describe the approach used by the commercial banking institution to determine
the geographic and socioeconomic distribution of its lending, investing, and
services.  Following are other specific components of the community
reinvestment plan required by the county:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1. 
Lending.  The plan shall provide information about:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                a. 
mortgage-lending efforts and products, including innovative loan products with
more flexible terms, and how these efforts and products are meeting the credit
needs of low and moderate income individuals and geographies;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                b. 
community development lending, by the commercial banking institution, that
helps develop affordable housing and helps nonprofit organizations and private
facilities expand job creation and support services for working families, with
the goal of increasing economic stability; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                c.  small
business lending, by the commercial banking institution, to establish
businesses and create jobs, particularly those in low-income and minority
census tracts that foster and support minority and women owned businesses; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>               d. 
lending, by the commercial banking institution, to finance improvements to
public infrastructure within the county;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2. 
Investing.  The plan shall provide information about:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                a.  tax
credit investments, grants, corporate giving activities and nonprofit
sponsorships, by the commercial banking institution, that provide and promote
affordable housing, encourage self-sufficiency and foster economic development;
and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>   b.  grants and
corporate giving activities, by the commercial banking institution, that
provide or promote improvements to public infrastructure within the county; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3. 
Services.  The plan shall provide information about:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                a. 
programs and community partnerships of the commercial banking institution that
provide small-business development, workforce development, capacity building
and asset development;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                b. 
community services involving commercial banking institution employees,
including participation in consumer training or seminars, home-buyer seminars,
tax-preparation services and financial education for students and serving on
nonprofit boards and committees;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                c.  branch
networks, including a record of opening and closing of branch offices and
information about whether these changes have had a positive or negative effect,
particularly in low- and moderate-income geographies; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                 d. 
services and efforts of the commercial banking institution that address the
credit needs of low- and moderate-income individuals and geographies,
including, but not limited to, affordable check cashing, alternatives to payday
loans and prepaid debit cards and other outreach and education opportunities
aimed at assisting distressed home mortgages.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            H.  The community
reinvestment plan shall include clear and measurable performance goals or
standards to demonstrate that the commercial banking institution is making good
faith efforts to follow through on commitments and elements of the plan
referenced in subsection F. of this section.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            I.  The manager
of finance and business operations division shall reject a bid as nonresponsive
for failure to include every component of this section in the community
reinvestment plan submitted by a commercial banking institution.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            J.  The commercial
banking institution awarded the financial services contract shall provide a
plan for reaching out to individuals with limited English proficiency and to
provide such individuals with lending, investing and community banking
services.  (Ord. 19637 § 4, 2023:  Ord. 17756 § 3, 2014:  Ord. 14199 § 63,
2001:  Ord. 12076 § 42, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><b><span lang=DE>            4.14.048 
Annual certification of county's commercial banking institution.</span></b></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>            A.  The
manager of the finance and business operations division shall annually certify
to the council that the county's commercial banking institution has:</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>              1.  Made
good-faith efforts to comply with all goals and commitments contained within
the community reinvestment plan.  If the commercial banking institution and the
manager agree that an element cannot reasonably be achieved, the council shall
be notified within seven days of the agreement that the element cannot
reasonably be achieved;</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>              2. 
Maintained its &quot;outstanding&quot; Community Reinvestment Act rating under
K.C.C.4.14.040.C; and</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>              3. 
Complied with all provisions of the financial services contract.</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>            B.  The
certification, and any notice that an agreement that an element cannot be
reasonably achieved as determined under subsection A.1. of this section, shall
be electronically filed with the clerk of the council, who shall retain the
original and provide an electronic copy to all councilmembers, the council
chief of staff, the chief policy officer, and the lead staff for the budget and
fiscal management committee or its successor.  (Ord. 19637 § 5, 2023:  Ord.
17756 </span><span lang=DE>§ </span><span lang=DE>5, 2014).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>4.14.050  Approved
specifications forwarding - preproposal conference.</b>  The approved specifications
shall be forwarded to all commercial banking institutions capable of serving
the county's financial service needs and shall be made available to any commercial
banking institution or individual interested in providing the services and that
also qualifies as a Washington state public depository under RCW 36.48.060.  A
conference shall be arranged before submission of formal proposals to receive
input from the commercial banking institution community on the approved
specifications and answer any questions related to the approved specifications. 
(Ord. 19637 § 6, 2023:  Ord. 17756 § 6, 2014:  Ord. 12076 § 43, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><b><span lang=DE>            4.14.070 
County financial service contracts - waiver prohibited - extension and
termination - actions upon breach.</span></b></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>            Except for
financial services contracts authorized by</span><span lang=DE> </span><span
lang=DE>K.C.C. 4.14.080 or as otherwise provided by this chapter, the manager
of the finance and business operations division may not waive the requirements
in this chapter for the county financial services contract.  In administering
the program, the manager shall:</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>            A.  Have
the option to extend the existing financial services contract by up to one
calendar year should no commercial banking institutions meet the specifications
through the request for proposals process.  The manager shall notify the
council seven days after extending the financial services contract.  The executive
shall electronically file the notification as required in this subsection A. of
this section with the clerk of the council, who shall retain the original and provide
an electronic copy to all councilmembers, the council chief of staff, the chief
policy officer, and the lead staff of the budget and fiscal management
committee, or its successor.  Any changes to the financial services
specifications and the evaluation criteria shall follow the process as required
by K.C.C. 4.14.040;</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>            B. 
Include a clause in the financial services contract allowing the county to
terminate the contract with one hundred eighty days' notice should the commercial
banking institution, at any time during the contract, lose its &quot;outstanding&quot;
Community Reinvestment Act rating under K.C.C. 4.14.040.C;</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>            C. 
Exercise the county's option to terminate under subsection B. of this section
if the commercial banking institution fails to cure as specified in the
contract or subsection D. of this section, and notify the council within seven
days of sending the notice to the commercial banking institution should the
commercial banking institution under contract with the county lose its &quot;outstanding&quot;
Community Reinvestment Act rating under K.C.C. 4.14.040.C. during the course of
the contract.  The executive shall electronically file the notification
required in this subsection C. of this section with the clerk of the council,
who shall retain the original and provide an electronic copy to all
councilmembers, the council chief of staff, the chief policy officer, and the
lead staff of the budget and fiscal management committee, or its successor;</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>            D.  If the
commercial banking institution does not comply with any provision of the financial
services contract:</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>              1.  Upon
finding a first breach of the financial services contract, immediately inform
the commercial banking institution of the breach and offer the commercial
banking institution ninety days to cure the breach.  However if the bank
receives a Community Reinvestment Act rating from the U.S. Office of the
Comptroller of the Currency, the U.S. Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve
Bank, and the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation that is below &quot;outstanding&quot;
for the assessment area that includes the geographical boundaries of King
County, the manager of the finance and business operations division has the
discretion to allow the commercial banking institution to cure that problem by
requesting that the commercial banking institution immediately update its
community reinvestment plan to address specific deficiencies noted in the
rating process; and provided further that, the outstanding Community
Reinvestment Act rating must be reinstated by the U.S. Office of the
Comptroller of the Currency, the U.S. Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve
Bank, and the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation within two years from the
date of the rating downgrade;</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>              2. 
After ninety days after a first breach, if the commercial banking institution
has not corrected the breach, issue a one-hundred-eighty-day termination
notice;</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>              3.  If
the commercial banking institution breaches the contract a second time during
the term, immediately inform the commercial banking institution of a second
breach and require of the commercial banking institution a payment to the
county equal to five percent of the previous year's total commercial banking
fees incurred by the county.  The commercial banking institution then has
ninety days to correct the breach.  The county may then use the penalty funds
to contract with a nonprofit organization or organizations to provide services
increasing access to consumer credit, subject to appropriation;</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>              4.  If,
after ninety days of a second breach, the commercial banking institution has
not corrected the breach, issue a one-hundred-eighty-day termination notice;</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>              5.  If a
commercial banking institution breaches the contract a third time during the
term, immediately inform the commercial banking institution of a second breach
and require from the commercial banking institution a payment equal to ten
percent of the previous year's total commercial banking fees incurred by the
county.  The county may then use the penalty funds to contract with a nonprofit
organization or organizations increasing access to consumer credit, subject to
appropriation.  The commercial banking institution then has ninety days to
correct the breach;</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>              6.  If,
after ninety days of a third breach, the commercial banking institution has not
corrected the problem that led to the breach, issue a one-hundred-eighty-day
termination notice; and</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>              7.  If the
commercial banking institution breaches the contract for a fourth time during
the term, immediately issue a one-hundred-eighty-day termination notice,  The
manager shall notify the council of any breach of contract and the action taken
by the county as required in this subsection D. of this section within seven
days of the breach of contract.  The executive shall electronically file the
notifications with the clerk of the council, who shall retain the original and
provide an electronic copy to all councilmembers, the chief of staff, the chief
policy officer, and the lead staff of the budget and fiscal management
committee or its successor; and</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>            E. 
Arrange for the commercial banking institution to make a presentation to the
county's executive finance committee, at least once a year that includes an
update of the community reinvestment plan and a report on the progress of
fulfilling commitments in the community reinvestment plan.  (Ord. 19637 § 7,
2023:  Ord. 19297 § 3, 2021:  Ord. 17756 </span><span lang=DE>§ </span><span
lang=DE>7, 2014).</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><b><span lang=DE>            4.14.080 
Financial services contract with another commercial banking institution that is
not the county's main financial services commercial banking institution for
local government convenience.</span></b></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>            The county
may enter into a financial services contract with another commercial banking
institution that is not the county's main financial services commercial banking
institution for the convenience of one or more local governments for which the
county serves as treasurer.  In these circumstances, the financial services contract
shall be paid for by the local government or governments and the manager of the
finance and business operations division may waive portions of the requirements
contained within this chapter to provide a convenience to the local government
or governments.  (Ord. 19637 § 8, 2023:  Ord. 17756 </span><span lang=DE>§ </span><span
lang=DE>8, 2014).</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><b><span lang=DE>            4.14.090 
Chapter not basis for proviate right of action.</span></b></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>            This
chapter is a general statement of county policy that cannot form the basis of a
private right of action.  (Ord. 17756 </span><span lang=DE>§ </span><span
lang=DE>9, 2014).</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc319916843"></a><a name="_Toc319916905"></a><a
name="_Toc412720788"><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>4.24    EXECUTIVE FINANCE
COMMITTEE AND INTERFUND BORROWING</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.24.010      Executive finance committee
designated agency to provide for interfund borrowing.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.24.020      Rules for temporary transfer of
funds.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.24.030      Report of interfund borrowing.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.24.010  Executive
finance committee designated agency to provide for interfund borrowing.</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>
Executive Order No. 1008 establishes an executive finance committee and defines
its membership and responsibilities as to the judicious investment of county
treasury funds and the redemption of county securities, and is hereby
designated as the agency authorized to provide for interfund borrowing.  (Ord.
823 § 1, 1971).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.24.020  Rules
for temporary transfer of funds.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  In accordance with
authority established by Section 490 of the Home Rule Charter, the executive
finance committee shall adopt rules and procedures which pertain to the
transfer of funds on a temporary loan basis from one solvent county fund to
another solvent county fund.  Such rules shall provide the duration and
interest rate to be charged on such loans.  (Ord. 823 § 2, 1971).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.24.030  Report
of interfund borrowing.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  Each month following the regular
meeting of the executive finance committee, it shall file with the county
council a current report of all interfund borrowing including the funds
involved, the amounts of the loans authorized and outstanding, the terms of the
loans and the interest charges, if any.  (Ord. 823 § 3, 1971).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc412720789"></a><a name="_Toc319916844"></a><a
name="_Toc319916906"><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>4.26    VEHICLE LICENSE FEE</span></a><span
style='font-size:12.0pt'>*</span></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>*Reviser's
note:  K.C.C. 4.26.010, 4.26.015 and 4.26.020 were recodified as a new chapter
in K.C.C. Title 4A.  However, the state section authorizing the chapter, RCW
82.80.020, was repealed in 2003. Therefore, the sections were not recodified,
pending an ordinance to dispose of the sections.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.26.005      Definitions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.26.010      Fee levied.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.26.015      Exemptions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.26.020      Collection.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.26.005  Definitions.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            </span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>A. 
&quot;Combined disposable household income&quot; shall mean the disposable
income of the person applying for the exemption plus the disposable income of
all persons in the household.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  &quot;Disposable
income&quot; shall mean the same as the term is defined by RCW 84.36.383(6) as
now or hereafter amended.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  &quot;Physical
disability&quot; shall mean the same as the term is defined by RCW 46.16.381(1)
as now or hereafter amended.  (Ord. 11123 § 1, 1993).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.26.010  Fee
levied.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  Under the authority of RCW 82.80.020, there is hereby
levied an annual fee of fifteen dollars per vehicle for each vehicle authorized
by RCW 82.80.020(1) and determined by the Washington state Department of Licensing to be registered within the boundaries of the county.  (Ord. 14226 § 1, 2001: 
Ord. 9736, 1990:  Ord. 9735 § 1, 1990).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.26.015  Exemptions.</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  The
registered owners of vehicles residing within the boundaries of the county who,
at the time payment of the fee established by this chapter is due, are
sixty-one years old or older and whose combined disposable household income is
seventy percent or less of the state median as determined by the Washington state
Office of Financial Management or have a permanent physical disability and have
been issued a permanent disabled persons placard or disabled person's license
plates by the Washington state Department of Licensing shall, upon application,
be exempted from this fee.  Proof of disability must be provided.  (Ord. 17292 </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>
5, 2012:  Ord. 11123 § 2, 1993).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.26.020  Collection.</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 
The fee imposed by this chapter shall be collected and administered by the
Washington state Department of Licensing; the revenues from the fee imposed by
this chapter shall be distributed to the county and the cities; and the
proceeds of the fee imposed by this chapter shall be used strictly for
transportation purposes in accordance with Chapter 42, Laws of Washington 1990. 
(Ord. 17292 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 7, 2012:  Ord. 9736, 1990: Ord. 9735 § 2, 1990).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc319916852"></a><a name="_Toc319916914"></a><a
name="_Toc412720790"><span class=MsoLineNumber><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>4.35    EXCISE TAX ON </span></span></a><span
style='font-size:12.0pt'>TIMBER<span class=MsoLineNumber><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'> HARVESTED FROM PUBLIC LANDS</span></span></span></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span class=MsoLineNumber><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.35.010      Tax imposed - payment,
collection and remittal.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.35.020      Tax rates - excuse of tax.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.35.030      Administration and collection.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.35.040      Distribution of revenues.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.35.050      Contract for administration and
collection.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.35.060      Inspection of county records
authorized to facilitate administration.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.35.070      Violations - misdemeanor.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span class=MsoLineNumber><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span class=MsoLineNumber><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>4.35.010 
Tax imposed - payment, collection and remittal.</b>  </span></span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>There is hereby
imposed, as authorized by RCW 84.33.051(1), an excise tax on every person
engaging in business in the county of King as a harvester as defined by RCW
84.33.035.  The tax shall be paid, collected and remitted to the Department of
Revenue of the state of Washington at the time and in the matter prescribed by
RCW<u> </u>84.33.086 and regulations adopted thereunder.  (<span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 15135 § 2, 2005:  Ord. 6942 § 1, 1984).</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>4.35.020  Tax rates - excuse of
tax.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.  For timber harvested from
privately owned land, the tax imposed by K.C.C. 4.35.010 shall be equal to the
stumpage value of the timber, as defined in RCW 84.33.035, multiplied by the
rate of four percent.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  For timber harvested from
publicly owned land, t<span class=MsoLineNumber><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>he
tax shall be equal to the stumpage value of the timber, as defined in RCW
84.33.035, multiplied by the following rates:</span></span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span class=MsoLineNumber><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  For
timber harvested January 1, 2005, through December 31, 2005, 1.2 percent;</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span class=MsoLineNumber><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  For
timber harvested January 1, 2006, through December 31, 2006, 1.5 percent;</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span class=MsoLineNumber><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  For
timber harvested January 1, 2007, through December 31, 2007, 1.8 percent;</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span class=MsoLineNumber><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              4.  For
timber harvested January 1, 2008, through December 31, 2008, 2.1 percent;</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span class=MsoLineNumber><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              5.  For
timber harvested January 1, 2009, through December 31, 2009, 2.4 percent;</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span class=MsoLineNumber><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              6.  For
timber harvested January 1, 2010, through December 31, 2010, 2.7 percent;</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span class=MsoLineNumber><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              7.  For
timber harvested January 1, 2011, through December 31, 2011, 3.1 percent;</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span class=MsoLineNumber><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              8.  For
timber harvested January 1, 2012, through December 31, 2012, 3.4 percent;</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span class=MsoLineNumber><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              9.  For
timber harvested January 1, 2013, through December 31, 2013, 3.7 percent; and</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span class=MsoLineNumber><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              10.  For
timber harvested January 1, 2014, and thereafter, 4.0 percent.</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span class=MsoLineNumber><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            C.  </span></span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Any harvester, as
defined in RCW 84.33.035, incurring less than fifty dollars liability in any
calendar quarter in excused from the payment of the tax imposed by Ordinance
6942, Section 1, but may be required by the Department of Revenue to file a
return even though no tax may be due.  (<span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord.
15135 § 3, 2005:  Ord. 6942 § 3, 1984).</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>4.35.030  Administration and
collection.</b>  The administration and collection of the tax imposed by this
chapter shall be in accordance with chapters 82.32 and 84.33 RCW, including
penal provisions, with respect to the tax imposed by Ordinance 6942, except
that RCW 82.32.045 and RCW 82.32.270 shall not apply.  (<span style='letter-spacing:
-.1pt'>Ord. 15135 § 4, 2005:  Ord. 6942 § 4, 1984).</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>4.35.040  Distribution of
revenues.</b>  Revenue distributed to the county by the state Treasurer from
the proceeds of the tax imposed by K.C.C. 4.35.010, shall be deposited in the
county timber tax account as established by the finance and business operations
division of the department of executive services.  Within thirty days after
receiving the revenues from the state Treasurer, the director of finance and
business operations division of the department of executive services shall make
distribution from the revenues to taxing districts in the county, except the
state, as prescribed by RCW 84.33.081.  (<span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord.
15135 § 5, 2005:  Ord. 6942 § 5, 1984).</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>4.35.050 Contract for
administration and collection.</b>  The county executive is hereby authorized
to contract with the Department of Revenue for the administration and
collection of the tax imposed by K.C.C. 4.35.010, and to provide in the
agreement for the payment from the tax proceeds of the costs of collection and
administration incurred by the Department of Revenue as directed in RCW
84.33.051.  (<span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 15135 § 6, 2005:  Ord.
6942 § 6, 1984).</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>4.35.060  Inspection of county
records authorized to facilitate administration.</b>  <span
class=MsoLineNumber><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>The county
agrees to give to the state Department of Revenue such facts and information
and to permit the Department of Revenue to inspect the county's records in
connection with the imposition, collection and administration of the tax
imposed by K.C.C. 4.35.010, as may be necessary to permit the Department of
Revenue to facilitate the administration of the tax and ensure the correct
distribution of its proceeds as provided by chapters 82.32 and 84.33 RCW.  </span></span>(<span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 15135 § 7, 2005:  Ord. 6942 § 7, 1984).</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>4.35.070  Violations -
misdemeanor.</b>  Any person responsible for payment of the tax imposed by this
chapter who fails to pay amounts lawfully due, and any person engaging in an
unlawful act as defined in RCW 82.32.290 is guilty of a misdemeanor.  (<span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 15135 § 8, 2005:  Ord. 6942 § 8, 1984).</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc319916853"></a><a name="_Toc319916915"></a><a
name="_Toc412720791"><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>4.36    COUNTY PROPERTY &#8209;
PAYMENT OF RENT</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.36.010      Rental payments.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.36.020      Record and deposit of
collections.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
